UNION vs UNION ALL in SQL
In SQL, UNION and UNION ALL operators are used to combine the results of two or more SELECT queries into a single result set.


1. UNION Operator
Definition (Interview Answer): UNION combines the results of multiple SELECT statements and removes duplicate rows.

Example:

Suppose we have two tables.

Table 1: Students_A
name
Rahul
Amit
Priya

Table 2: Students_B
name
Amit
Sneha
Rahul

*******************SQL query:
SELECT name FROM Students_A
UNION
SELECT name FROM Students_B;

*****************Output:
name
Rahul
Amit
Priya
Sneha

*********************************************************

2. UNION ALL Operator
Definition (Interview Answer): UNION ALL combines the results of multiple SELECT statements and keeps all duplicate rows.

SQL query:
SELECT name FROM Students_A
UNION ALL
SELECT name FROM Students_B;

**************Output:
name
Rahul
Amit
Priya
Amit
Sneha
Rahul

3. Difference between UNION and UNION ALL


UNION	                                                 UNION ALL
Removes duplicate rows                            	Keeps duplicate rows
Usually slower	                                        Usually faster
Performs duplicate elimination	                    Does not perform duplicate elimination
Use when unique results are required	            Use when all records are required

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Important rules
Both operators require:
1. The same number of columns in each SELECT.
2. Corresponding columns to have compatible data types.
3. The output column names generally come from the first SELECT.


